WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:02.460
- He's an American business magnet,

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.103
philanthropist, investor, and author.

00:00:05.103 --> 00:00:07.374
He's the founder and CEO of Dell Inc.

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:10.487
One of the world's leading
sellers of personal computers.

00:00:10.487 --> 00:00:14.842
Forbes estimates his net worth
to be 19.5 billion dollars.

00:00:14.842 --> 00:00:18.718
He's Michael Dell and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:21.523
(swoosh)

00:00:21.523 --> 00:00:26.164
- You know you have to think
about the point of impact.

00:00:26.164 --> 00:00:29.299
If you're running a
business with 100 people,

00:00:29.299 --> 00:00:32.292
the point of impact for the
leader is very different

00:00:32.292 --> 00:00:35.068
than if it's 1000 people
or 10,000, 100,000.

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:37.513
And so that's the frame of reference

00:00:37.513 --> 00:00:40.629
that I've always used to say okay,

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:42.121
if I'm leading this organization,

00:00:42.121 --> 00:00:45.132
what are the things that actually I can do

00:00:45.132 --> 00:00:47.311
to cause it to be successful?

00:00:47.311 --> 00:00:50.210
If it's 1000 people,
it's very, very different

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:51.609
than it is for me today.

00:00:51.609 --> 00:00:54.379
Today I'm a lot more focused on strategy,

00:00:54.379 --> 00:00:56.831
because that's the point of impact

00:00:56.831 --> 00:00:58.974
on 110,000 person company.

00:00:58.974 --> 00:01:01.980
On a hundred person company,
you're sort of doing

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.050
any job in the organization.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.137
You're doing every job in the
organization, potentially.

00:01:07.137 --> 00:01:09.388
And that might be okay at that point.

00:01:09.388 --> 00:01:12.140
One of the things I see in new businesses,

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.373
is they're often looking for perfection.

00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:16.369
And I think this is a really big mistake.

00:01:16.369 --> 00:01:20.041
I think you ought to be
looking for experiments.

00:01:20.041 --> 00:01:25.009
And quick experiments and it's
okay to have little failures.

00:01:25.009 --> 00:01:26.679
And what you want to do is learn

00:01:26.679 --> 00:01:28.480
as much as you can very quickly.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.590
And try things.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:33.500
And certainly when we
were a small business,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.926
you know, we'd go out and
try all sorts of things

00:01:35.926 --> 00:01:37.967
to find what would work.

00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:39.449
And then we found
something that would work,

00:01:39.449 --> 00:01:42.413
we'd do that 100 more
times or 1,000 more times.

00:01:42.413 --> 00:01:45.678
We still do that today as a large company.

00:01:45.678 --> 00:01:49.381
When I was 16, I got this job working

00:01:49.381 --> 00:01:51.631
for a newspaper in Houston.

00:01:52.599 --> 00:01:55.294
And my job was to sell subscriptions

00:01:55.294 --> 00:01:57.662
to the paper on the telephone.

00:01:57.662 --> 00:02:01.662
And I realized two things
when I was doing this.

00:02:05.251 --> 00:02:08.555
I realized that people that
were buying the newspaper

00:02:08.555 --> 00:02:11.082
generally had two things in common.

00:02:11.082 --> 00:02:13.954
Either they were moving
to a new residence,

00:02:13.954 --> 00:02:16.202
or they were getting married.

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:20.119
It turns out that you
could go find information

00:02:21.195 --> 00:02:24.882
about both of those things
in enormous quantities.

00:02:24.882 --> 00:02:28.389
So the state that I lived in, in Texas,

00:02:28.389 --> 00:02:30.542
when you want to get a marriage license,

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:35.398
you have to file with the state
and it's public information.

00:02:35.398 --> 00:02:38.975
Particularly the address
you want the license sent to

00:02:38.975 --> 00:02:41.151
once it's issued.

00:02:41.151 --> 00:02:45.318
So I hired all my friends,
and went to every county

00:02:46.398 --> 00:02:49.472
in the surrounding 16 counties in Houston,

00:02:49.472 --> 00:02:51.612
captured the addresses of all the people

00:02:51.612 --> 00:02:54.167
who applied for marriage licenses

00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:56.523
and sent them a direct mail offer

00:02:56.523 --> 00:03:00.136
to offer them the
newspaper for a free trial

00:03:00.136 --> 00:03:01.870
and then a subscription.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:06.037
And I ended up making a fair
some of money for a teenager.

00:03:08.029 --> 00:03:09.752
In our situation where we were

00:03:09.752 --> 00:03:12.400
creating a new way of doing business,

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.173
that was actually part
of our culture as well.

00:03:16.173 --> 00:03:19.006
And as we wanted to attract people

00:03:19.931 --> 00:03:22.808
from let's say related spaces,

00:03:22.808 --> 00:03:24.667
we didn't actually want them to bring

00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:26.783
the culture of the other companies.

00:03:26.783 --> 00:03:30.169
We might want some insights from their

00:03:30.169 --> 00:03:32.198
experience and that sort of thing.

00:03:32.198 --> 00:03:33.681
But we had to be very explicit about

00:03:33.681 --> 00:03:37.495
the Dell culture was really
all about, what the values are,

00:03:37.495 --> 00:03:39.824
particularly as we
expand around the world.

00:03:39.824 --> 00:03:44.104
You know, these aren't
necessarily American values,

00:03:44.104 --> 00:03:46.937
these are the Dell company values.

00:03:48.212 --> 00:03:49.885
This is incredibly important,

00:03:49.885 --> 00:03:53.493
and this is the way we
operate our business.

00:03:53.493 --> 00:03:56.396
One of the things that
people do instinctually

00:03:56.396 --> 00:03:59.219
when there are tough times,
is they kind of do nothing.

00:03:59.219 --> 00:04:00.406
(laughs)

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:03.555
And you know they kind of hunker down.

00:04:03.555 --> 00:04:04.787
- Is this sort of the frozen rabbits

00:04:04.787 --> 00:04:05.620
in the head lights thing.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.687
- You know, it's kind of
completely stunned into inaction,

00:04:09.687 --> 00:04:12.563
and I think that's actually
the worst thing to do.

00:04:12.563 --> 00:04:16.730
I think times when everyone is
confused and kind of stunned,

00:04:18.311 --> 00:04:21.091
present the most enormous opportunity,

00:04:21.091 --> 00:04:23.652
because no one is really doing anything.

00:04:23.652 --> 00:04:27.053
So, you know, I think this is a time

00:04:27.053 --> 00:04:30.791
where the seeds of really successful

00:04:30.791 --> 00:04:32.478
new businesses will be created.

00:04:32.478 --> 00:04:34.345
In fact, if you look in our industry,

00:04:34.345 --> 00:04:36.228
which I'm most familiar with, you know,

00:04:36.228 --> 00:04:40.082
the greatest companies
kind of came out of,

00:04:40.082 --> 00:04:42.171
and were formed in some of the

00:04:42.171 --> 00:04:44.090
most difficult times in the industry.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:45.786
When no one was really looking,

00:04:45.786 --> 00:04:47.636
and everyone really thought

00:04:47.636 --> 00:04:49.548
the whole thing was going to go south,

00:04:49.548 --> 00:04:52.743
all of a sudden a whole new
breed of companies emerged.

00:04:52.743 --> 00:04:56.545
So for example if you look
at when Dell was formed,

00:04:56.545 --> 00:04:59.106
in the early 1980s, 1984,

00:04:59.106 --> 00:05:03.345
this was pretty dark
time in the US economy.

00:05:03.345 --> 00:05:04.959
The personal computer industry

00:05:04.959 --> 00:05:07.648
had just gone through a big down cycle.

00:05:07.648 --> 00:05:09.566
And the prevailing wisdom was that

00:05:09.566 --> 00:05:13.397
all electronics would be
dominated by the Japanese.

00:05:13.397 --> 00:05:14.293
- Yeah.

00:05:14.293 --> 00:05:18.920
- That we had this big
boogie man called Japan Inc.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.192
And that no one could
compete with Japan Inc.

00:05:21.192 --> 00:05:24.120
Especially in something like computers.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.030
But it turned out to be completely wrong

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:28.896
and there was enormous opportunity,

00:05:28.896 --> 00:05:32.076
and huge waves of
productivity were unleashed,

00:05:32.076 --> 00:05:35.326
with the computer industry flourishing.

00:05:36.797 --> 00:05:39.127
Be willing to experiment and fail.

00:05:39.127 --> 00:05:42.337
A lot of people go into business

00:05:42.337 --> 00:05:44.065
and they're looking to
never make any mistakes.

00:05:44.065 --> 00:05:45.472
That's not the way to succeed.

00:05:45.472 --> 00:05:47.866
You have to be willing to
try things, experiment,

00:05:47.866 --> 00:05:51.583
ask a lot of questions, don't
be stuck on any one position.

00:05:51.583 --> 00:05:55.750
And most importantly, to listen
and look for breakthroughs,

00:05:57.259 --> 00:06:00.778
you know, and ideas that
haven't been out there.

00:06:00.778 --> 00:06:04.385
As a business grows, the
natural tendency would be

00:06:04.385 --> 00:06:08.232
to take fewer risks and to be cautious

00:06:08.232 --> 00:06:10.399
and to have contingency plans,

00:06:10.399 --> 00:06:12.488
but in business is changing very quickly,

00:06:12.488 --> 00:06:14.847
or one that you want to grow,

00:06:14.847 --> 00:06:19.749
you actually need to be
explicit about accepting risk.

00:06:19.749 --> 00:06:23.294
One of the people who work with me,

00:06:23.294 --> 00:06:24.757
kind of describe it this way.

00:06:24.757 --> 00:06:27.352
I was sort of encouraging this person,

00:06:27.352 --> 00:06:28.946
hey why let's try this,
why don't we do this,

00:06:28.946 --> 00:06:30.381
how about this?

00:06:30.381 --> 00:06:32.162
At some point he came back and said,

00:06:32.162 --> 00:06:34.159
"So, what you're telling my is

00:06:34.159 --> 00:06:36.900
"you want me to try to do ten things,

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.406
"and if I only get eight of them,

00:06:38.406 --> 00:06:42.907
"you'll be okay with that
if I don't get all ten."

00:06:42.907 --> 00:06:47.202
Versus, having five things
and doing all five perfectly.

00:06:47.202 --> 00:06:48.675
That's exactly right.

00:06:48.675 --> 00:06:51.592
We want to accept some experiments.

00:06:52.475 --> 00:06:55.290
If you just dissect the IT sector,

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.646
you know, look at the whole thing,

00:06:57.646 --> 00:07:01.479
you've got roughly three, three and a half

00:07:01.479 --> 00:07:02.794
trillion dollar industry.

00:07:02.794 --> 00:07:05.384
And there are about ten companies

00:07:05.384 --> 00:07:09.296
that have more than one
percent of the three trillion.

00:07:09.296 --> 00:07:10.524
We have two percent.

00:07:10.524 --> 00:07:13.220
But nobody has like more
than five or six percent.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:15.720
It's a quite fragmented space.

00:07:18.019 --> 00:07:21.686
So, we work extensively
with partners across

00:07:23.399 --> 00:07:27.566
the ecosystem and it's a big
part of how we evolve rapidly,

00:07:28.558 --> 00:07:31.266
go quickly, reach new customers,

00:07:31.266 --> 00:07:35.433
gain access to the latest
ingredient technologies.

00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:40.823
Whether it's in semi-conductors,
or software ingredients.

00:07:43.704 --> 00:07:45.684
So the partnerships and alliances

00:07:45.684 --> 00:07:49.698
are incredibly important
part of how we grow.

00:07:49.698 --> 00:07:53.060
If you really don't know what it means

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.251
to be an entrepreneur, may be you are one.

00:07:55.251 --> 00:07:58.639
So, I think there's a
bit of self initiative,

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:01.806
and self-starter that is an incredibly

00:08:03.042 --> 00:08:06.188
important part of entrepreneurship.

00:08:06.188 --> 00:08:08.349
And no one can tell you how to do it,

00:08:08.349 --> 00:08:12.807
you have to sort of have
an instinctual feeling,

00:08:12.807 --> 00:08:15.387
an idea about something.

00:08:15.387 --> 00:08:16.870
And you got to be passionate about it.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:21.037
I think people that look for
great ideas to make money,

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:25.797
aren't nearly as
successful as those who say

00:08:27.241 --> 00:08:30.292
what do I really love to
do, what am I excited about,

00:08:30.292 --> 00:08:32.189
what do I know something about.

00:08:32.189 --> 00:08:36.299
When we have had failures,
those have ultimately resulted

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:39.079
in some of the greatest
successes we've ever had.

00:08:39.079 --> 00:08:41.152
You talk about those,
particularly when someone

00:08:41.152 --> 00:08:42.733
comes to see you and they say,

00:08:42.733 --> 00:08:44.288
"Yeah, I'm feeling really bad,

00:08:44.288 --> 00:08:45.901
"because this didn't work out."

00:08:45.901 --> 00:08:48.421
Okay, well let me tell you
about some other things

00:08:48.421 --> 00:08:50.870
that didn't work out that
we learned a lot from.

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.916
And let's pick ourselves back up,

00:08:53.916 --> 00:08:57.852
and let's figure out
what we learned from this

00:08:57.852 --> 00:08:59.754
and how we go apply that next time.

00:08:59.754 --> 00:09:00.987
You know, when you're in the zone

00:09:00.987 --> 00:09:03.514
of doing things that no
one has ever done before,

00:09:03.514 --> 00:09:07.036
if you're only succeeding,
you're not taking enough risk.

00:09:07.036 --> 00:09:08.289
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:09:08.289 --> 00:09:11.461
I made this video because
one of you asked me to.

00:09:11.461 --> 00:09:12.869
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:09:12.869 --> 00:09:14.704
that you want me to profile next,

00:09:14.704 --> 00:09:15.961
leave it in the comments below,

00:09:15.961 --> 00:09:17.160
and I'll see what I can do.

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:18.980
I'd also love to know
which of Michael Dell's

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:21.312
top ten rules meant the most to you.

00:09:21.312 --> 00:09:23.808
Leave it in the comments, we're
going to enjoy the discussion.

00:09:23.808 --> 00:09:25.479
Thank you so much for watching.

00:09:25.479 --> 00:09:27.471
Continue to believe,
and we'll see you soon.

00:09:27.471 --> 00:09:29.554
(swoosh)

